---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ESSAYS, HOW_PATH, REPO, SITE_LAB, adrUrl, howUrl } from '../../lib/links';

// The essay the Private sandbox and No personal data principles map to (docs/domain.md, "The
// principle map"; ADR 0005, ADR 0006). Neither has a lab page yet, so the bakery explains them
// itself. Static, no island. Every claim about Cloudflare's behaviour is as its documentation
// stated it on the date in READ, and every number from the code names its file.
const READ = '2026-11-06';
const { title } = ESSAYS.sandbox;
const SOURCES = [
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/namespace/',
    title: 'Durable Object Namespace',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Durable Objects documentation',
    for: 'an object is addressed by an id made from a name with idFromName(), and named objects are the usual way to refer to one',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/sqlite-storage-api/',
    title: 'SQLite-backed Durable Object Storage',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Durable Objects documentation',
    for: 'deleteAll() deletes all of an object’s stored data, SQL and key-value, atomically, and also an active alarm from a compatibility date of 2026-02-24; point-in-time recovery reaches back 30 days',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/IHaveASegway/website/blob/main/docs/lab.md',
    title: 'The site’s lab rules for every demo',
    by: 'ihaveasegway.com’s docs/lab.md',
    for: 'seeded or fixture data only, no real people, companies or addresses, no visitor free text in a first version, country or continent at most, and IP addresses only as a keyed hash with a salt made each UTC day and held in memory',
  },
  {
    href: `${REPO}/blob/main/docs/security.md`,
    title: 'Security (docs/security.md, as of 2026-11-05)',
    by: 'this repository',
    for: 'the isolation rules, the data inventory and the steps of the wipe',
  },
  {
    href: SITE_LAB,
    title: 'Lab',
    by: 'ihaveasegway.com',
    for: 'five lab pages, the Route, Queue, Cache, Backfill and Raft Labs, and none on a private sandbox or on keeping no personal data',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`${title} | Proof of Concept Bakery`}
  description="How Proof of Concept Bakery can be handed to a stranger: every object’s name carries its sandbox, the seed makes everything up, sign-in keeps only a keyed hash, and the only free text is checked as it is written."
>
  <article class="essay prose">
    <p class="hint">
      <a href={HOW_PATH}>How it’s built</a>
    </p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A demo that anyone may change, and that a recruiter may open on a borrowed laptop, has to be
      safe for the visitor and for the next one. This is how every sandbox is kept apart, why the
      bakery holds no personal data because it has nowhere to put any, and what that costs: a bakery
      in which nothing is real, the customers who open campaigns included.
    </p>

    <h2>A bakery handed to a stranger</h2>
    <p>
      A visitor signs in, opens the console and changes the price of the cardamom bun. Another
      visitor, at the same minute, cancels every order on the board. A third types a phone number
      into an order note. None of them should see the others’ work, and nobody should be left
      holding something that names a person. The bakery is a business system, and business systems
      are full of customers’ names, addresses and notes (<code>docs/adr/0005</code>), so a demo that
      works like one has to decide where each of those goes.
    </p>

    <h2>What a simpler design does, and where it breaks</h2>
    <p>
      The shared demo is the usual first design: one bakery, one database, every visitor in it.
      Anything one person types is shown to the next, so it needs moderation, and anything personal
      that lands in it stays until someone finds it. The second design keeps one shared set of
      tables and adds a tenant column to every row, with a filter on every query. One query without
      its filter shows every visitor everything, and a wipe has to find the rows by that column. No
      lab page shows this one yet. The{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        lab’s index
      </a>{' '}
      is where the lab explains the family of techniques around it, and the site’s rules for every
      demo, read on {READ}, are what the bakery keeps: made-up data, no free text at first, and
      nothing that outlives the visit.
    </p>

    <h2>A name that carries its sandbox</h2>
    <p>
      The bakery has no shared table to filter. A sandbox is a set of Durable Objects, each with its
      own SQLite database, and every object’s name is built by the Worker from the sandbox id in the
      signed session or token: <code>catalog:&lt;sandbox&gt;</code>,{' '}
      <code>schedule:&lt;sandbox&gt;:&lt;date&gt;</code>,{' '}
      <code>order:&lt;sandbox&gt;:&lt;ulid&gt;</code> (<code>docs/architecture.md</code>, “Object
      naming”). A client never names an object. Cloudflare’s namespace documentation, read on {READ}
      , addresses an object by an id derived from its name, so a name with another sandbox’s id is
      another object. A path that names another sandbox gets the 404 of a name that exists nowhere,
      and each object stores its sandbox id when it is born and refuses a mismatch.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Born only through the Sandbox.</strong> The <code>Sandbox</code> object keeps a
      registry of its children (<code>workers/api/src/objects/sandbox.rs</code>). A child is written
      into the registry before it is first written, in the same request, and the Sandbox hands it a{' '}
      <code>Birth</code> (<code>child.rs</code>): its name, the sandbox id and the clock. An object
      with no <code>meta</code> row refuses every command with 410, so a guessed name or a request
      after the wipe summons nothing. That is why the wipe has one list to work from, and it cannot
      miss an object the sandbox created.
    </p>

    <h2>Made up, and the same every time</h2>
    <p>
      <code>crates/seed</code> builds the whole starting state from the sandbox’s seed and birth
      date with a seeded ChaCha20 generator: 60 customers, 6 venues, 64 delivery places, 90 days of
      closed orders, 12 open orders and 8 catering events (<code>crates/seed/src/lib.rs</code>). Two
      sandboxes from one seed and date are identical byte for byte, so a bug report can name its
      seed. A customer is a made-up word pair, a plant or a weather word with a landform or a craft,
      such as Fennel Talus (<code>words.rs</code>). A delivery address is not an address at all:{' '}
      <code>p1</code> to <code>p64</code>, mapped onto the public places of the map by index. No
      free text is seeded.
    </p>

    <h2>No personal data is an absence of places to put it</h2>
    <p>
      The bakery does not promise to be careful with personal data. It has no column for it. The{' '}
      <code>Account</code> object’s tables hold an id, a kind, dates, the current sandbox, the ids
      of revoked sessions, and a demo account’s generated username and password hash (
      <code>account.rs</code>). Sign-in stores the id as a keyed hash: <code>HMAC-SHA256</code> of
      the provider’s number for the account, with a key held as a Worker secret (
      <code>docs/adr/0006</code>). Google is asked for the <code>openid</code> scope alone and
      GitHub for no scope, and if a provider ever sends an email, a name or a picture, the callback
      drops them before anything is stored or logged.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Mail has nowhere to go.</strong> The bakery has no address to send from or to, so
      every message is a row in the sandbox’s <code>Inbox</code>, whose recipient is a customer id
      and not an address. The Inbox’s own event, <code>MessageDelivered</code>, carries its
      recipient as <code>to: Ulid</code>, so there is no field an address could go in, and a
      recipient read from text must be a 26-character ULID or the event does not decode (
      <code>crates/domain/src/inbox.rs</code>, <code>crates/domain/src/id.rs</code>,{' '}
      <code>docs/security.md</code>).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Two free-text fields, checked as written.</strong> The order note is 140 characters
      and the catering brief 500 (<code>crates/domain/src/free_text.rs</code>). Both are normalised
      to NFC and then refused, with a rule such as <code>brief-email</code> or{' '}
      <code>brief-phone</code> and never the text, when they hold an email shape, 7 or more digits,
      13 to 19 digits or a URL. The consumer removes both from every event headed for the CRM or the
      Inbox. <strong>Logs</strong> carry ids, counts and slugs, and a request’s address only as{' '}
      <code>HMAC(daily salt, IP)</code>; the salt is made in memory and never written, because
      Cloudflare, as read on {READ}, can restore deleted storage for 30 days.
    </p>

    <h2>The wipe, from the one list</h2>
    <p>
      At 10:00 UTC a cron asks <code>Control</code> to stamp each sandbox due, and{' '}
      <code>Control</code> wipes them 50 a run from its own alarm (<code>wipe.rs</code>,{' '}
      <code>control.rs</code>). Each Sandbox wipes its registered children, deletes its alarm and
      runs <code>deleteAll()</code>; <code>Billing</code> first cancels, voids and deletes the
      Stripe objects it created, and each run ends in the <code>wipe</code> data point. Every
      object’s own 36-hour backstop covers a wipe that fails. Deleted is not erased: Cloudflare’s
      documentation says recovery reaches back 30 days, and nothing here calls it.
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing persists.</strong> A sandbox born at 09:55 UTC lasts five minutes (
        <code>wipe.rs</code>, tested), and the work of a visitor is gone by the next 10:00.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing is real.</strong> The customers who open campaigns are word pairs, and the
        opens and clicks are drawn by the Inbox’s alarms from the seed. The demo shows how the
        machinery behaves, not how customers do.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rows to seed and to wipe.</strong> About 2,500 rows written for a 20-minute session,
        2,000 of them at seed, and a wipe that deletes about as many again (
        <code>docs/adr/0005</code>, estimates as of 2026-11-04).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A filter, not a guarantee.</strong> A visitor can still type a name into a note. It
        stays in their own sandbox and goes with it, as <code>docs/security.md</code> says.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Open <a href="/app/">the console</a>: it shows your sandbox’s id, its seed and when it will be
      wiped, and Wipe now ends it on the spot. Read <a href="/privacy/">the privacy page</a>, then
      the answer to <code>GET /v1/session</code>, which is the whole of what the bakery knows about
      you. In the catering form, type a made-up email into the brief and read the refusal: it names
      the rule and does not repeat what you typed.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      {SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <a href={s.href} rel="noopener">
            {s.title}
          </a>
          , {s.by}, read on {READ}: {s.for}.
        </li>
      ))}
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://developers.google.com/identity/openid-connect/openid-connect"
          rel="noopener"
        >
          OpenID Connect
        </a>
        , Google Identity documentation, not read on {READ}; cited as <code>docs/adr/0006</code>{' '}
        records it on 2026-11-04: the <code>sub</code> claim is unique and never reused, and the{' '}
        <code>email</code> and <code>profile</code> claims come only with their scopes.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="hint">
      Back to <a href={howUrl('sandbox')}>the sandbox principle on How it’s built</a>, to{' '}
      <a href={howUrl('no-personal-data')}>no personal data</a>, to the decisions that chose them,{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0005')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0005
      </a>{' '}
      and{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0006')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0006
      </a>
      , or to{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        the lab
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
